# Copy to `.env.local` and fill in, or leave the file out entirely.
#
# Everything here is public. Nothing in this app is a secret, because
# `output: "export"` means there is no server to keep one in: these values are
# readable by anyone who opens the page and none of them authorises anything on
# its own.
#
# `NEXT_PUBLIC_` is what makes a value reach the browser under Next, and a static
# export bakes it in at build time, so changing any of these means a rebuild.


# THE CHAIN

# Which network this build runs against. One of `robinhood-testnet` or
# `robinhood-mainnet`, the keys in `src/config/networks.ts`.
#
# Left unset it is testnet, and the asymmetry is deliberate: a build that forgot
# to name a network runs against the chain where a mistake costs nothing, and
# reaching mainnet takes somebody typing its name.
#
# A name this build does not know fails `npm run build` rather than falling back
# to the default. A typo that quietly landed on testnet in a build meant for
# mainnet would read an empty pool, and an empty pool looks exactly like an
# account with no money in it.
NEXT_PUBLIC_COWL_NETWORK=robinhood-testnet


# SIGN IN

# Without the two ids below the app still runs and every screen behind the door
# is still reachable with `SKIP_LOGIN` in `src/config/ui.ts`. What you lose is
# the Google button, which reports that this build has no credentials rather
# than failing at Google's door with a page nobody can read.
#
# Both are public identifiers and neither authorises anything on its own: the
# auth proxy checks the request origin against an allowlist in Turnkey's
# dashboard, and Google checks the redirect uri against one in Google's.

# Turnkey · Dashboard · Embedded Wallets · Configuration · Auth Proxy Config ID.
# In that same panel: allowlist this app's origin, enable Google, and turn OFF
# every method you are not using. Email OTP left on is a second door into the
# same shielded account, and a weaker one than the Google account it sits beside.
NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNKEY_AUTH_PROXY_CONFIG_ID=

# Google Cloud · APIs and Services · Credentials · OAuth 2.0 Client ID, type Web
# application. It needs both:
#   Authorised JavaScript origins:  http://localhost:3000
#   Authorised redirect URIs:       http://localhost:3000/auth/callback
# Google compares these as strings, so the scheme, the port and the trailing
# slash all have to match exactly what the browser sends.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=

# Optional. Where Google returns the sign in popup to. Left blank it is this
# origin's `/auth/callback`, which is right for a laptop and for any deployment
# served from the host being visited. Set it when a build is served from a host
# it cannot read off `window.location`.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI=
